Someone mentioned using ` { watch: [locale] }`, but it results in an infinite load.
```js
const { data: page } = await useAsyncData('home', () => {
    return prismic.client.getSingle('home', {
        graphQuery: `{ ...truncated... }`,
        lang: `${locale.value}-ca`,
    }, { watch: [locale] });
});
```

![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/83cd32fe-00a5-4a9e-85f5-7ea2298191e3)

A working solution is to use a dynamic key like so:
```js
const { data: page } = await useAsyncData(`home-${locale.value}`, () => {
    return prismic.client.getSingle('home', {
        graphQuery: `{ ...truncated... }`,
        lang: `${locale.value}-ca`,
    });
});
```

> A working solution is to use a dynamic key like so:
> 
> const { data: page } = await useAsyncData(`home-${locale.value}`, () => {
>     return prismic.client.getSingle('home', {
>         graphQuery: `{ ...truncated... }`,
>         lang: `${locale.value}-ca`,
>     });
> });

This is the correct approach, otherwise you will be hitting localized cached results for `home` key. The previous usage with a static key did work before [Nuxt `3.17.0`](https://github.com/nuxt/nuxt/releases/tag/v3.17.0), which fixed some unintended behaviors (we had the same usage in our tests originally, see https://github.com/nuxt-modules/i18n/commit/cbfaa25ae9cc26c7345349c8d57adb018ef70e6c).

Perhaps this case could be added to the documentation somewhere, PR welcome of course 🙏

@BobbieGoede Wouldn't it be better to use watch: [locale], in order when the language is changing I get the newest version from my API? And only one key is cached (the generated one).

```
const { data: page } = await useAsyncData(() => {
    return prismic.client.getSingle('home', {
        graphQuery: `{ ...truncated... }`,
        lang: `${locale.value}-ca`,
    }, { watch: [locale] });
});
```

@martinsjek 
The getter/computed key should still trigger a refetch on change, you can also use the other parameters (like `watch`) if further fine tuning is needed https://nuxt.com/docs/api/composables/use-async-data#params.

The key is used for request deduplication(server/client) and caching, for this to work the key should be unique https://nuxt.com/docs/getting-started/data-fetching#useasyncdata and https://nuxt.com/docs/getting-started/data-fetching#caching-and-refetching. In your use case it sounds like you actually don't want caching, so I recommend looking into invalidating/refreshing the cache while using unique keys to avoid unexpected behavior.

> I don't see why changing locale should always invalidate cache, not all fetch requests are dependent on localization.

True, but I already pass the locale to the graphQuery, so maybe `useAsyncData` should automatically track every variable just like `watchEffect` does ?

```diff
const { data: page } = await useAsyncData('home', () => {
    return prismic.client.getSingle('home', {
        graphQuery: `{ ...truncated... }`,
+       lang: `${locale.value}-ca`,
    });
});
```

I have the exact same problem! Thanks for raising the issue.

I used a global middleware:
```
export default defineNuxtRouteMiddleware((to) => {
    const app = useNuxtApp();
    // Setting the i18n locale because we use no_prefix strategy
    app.$i18n.locale.value = getCountryCodeFromUrl(to.path) as 'at' | 'de';
});
```

Do you think this is a valid strategy? It works for my setup... but I know you shouldn't set the locale directly, but I didn't found another way.

How do you solve it right now?

@dargmuesli 

Could you create an issue in the nuxt security repo so that we can continue from there?

Maybe you already have an idea on how to fix it? I am happy to help with that :)

