Hi Simon,

I believe you'd like to import `Content` from `@prismicio/client` instead, which namespaces all your documents and slice types. Could you try something like:

```ts
imports: {
	imports: [
		{ name: "Content", from: "@prismicio/client", type: true }
	]
}
```

This should allow you to reference all types from `prismicio-types.d.ts` as `Content.*` in your app.

By the way, since `@nuxtjs/prismic` v5 introduced `@prismicio/client` as a peer dependency, I'm considering adding auto imports for its helpers (`asImageSrc`, `asHTML`, etc.) and types (`Content`, mainly), but I'm worried about naming collisions. If you have any opinion/wish around that, let me know!

Hi @danielaag, yes, exactly! As part of the latest majors, only `client` and `isFilled` remain available from `usePrismic()`: https://prismic.io/docs/technical-reference/prismicio-vue/v6#update-useprismic-and-prismic-usage

Here's the reasoning behind it:
- `import { ... } from "@prismicio/client"` vs. `const { ... } = usePrismic()` is roughly the same amount of code inside your script sections (your IDE should also be smarter at writing the import for you than figuring out it has to extract it from a composable, even though AI completion changes that a bit...)
- `asLink`, `asText`, etc, were *mostly* just passthrough from `@prismicio/client` through the Vue plugin. Removing them allowed for simplifying internal code while also removing some of the "magic" (importing from a package is more "explicit" than extracting from a composable)
- Only `isFilled` was preserved to ease templating (through `$prismic.isFilled`) as it's the only helper that doesn't have a component equivalent (which are auto-imported), and in most cases, you shouldn't need other helpers for templating (but if you have counter examples, happy to discuss that!)

What I'm considering is whether or not the Nuxt module should add auto imports for them, something like (which you can already configure yourself if you want to!):
```ts
imports: {
	presets: [
		{
			from: "@prismicio/client",
			imports: [
				{ name: "Content", type: true },
				"asText", "asLink", "asImageSrc", "..."
			]
		}
	]
}
```

My concerns being around naming collisions (so probably having to provide a toggle for it)

@lihbr i think thats nice and gives more control - thank you! The downside i see is that there could be name collisionns quite easily with something liek "asLink".

