I believe this is still an issue, I noticed it after updating nuxt and related dependencies across my site. Previously reactivity using `useSanityQuery` was working correctly.

Package versions:
- `nuxt`: 4.4.8
- `@nuxtjs/sanity`: 2.3.0

In my case this only happened in our production builds in Vercel, not in preview environments or local development. The bug also only occurred when navigating from a different route to the route that used `useSanityQuery`, if you started at the affected route, reactivity worked fine. This makes it hard to create a good reproduction.

This occurred on our site on pages which list things like news articles, and use route query parameters as the source of truth. Reactivity in the UI around changes to the route query parameters worked correctly, it was just the data from `useSanityQuery` that never seemed to update.

I attempted to workaround the issue by manually adding `route` to the `watch` list, but this didn't have any effect:

```js
const route = useRoute()
const routeFilterTopic = computed(() => route.query["topic"]);
const { data } = await useSanityQuery(
  newsQueries,
  {
    topic: routeFilterTopic,
  },
  {
    watch: [route], // no change to reactivity
  },
);
```

Looking at the source of useSanityQuery, I noticed that the `key` passed to `useAsyncData` was being constructed without reactivity: https://github.com/nuxt-modules/sanity/blob/main/src/runtime/composables/useSanityQuery.ts#L57

I replicated a very similar key construction in my page component and then passed that as the `key` option:

```js
const route = useRoute()
const routeFilterTopic = computed(() => route.query["topic"]);
const queryKey = computed(
  () => 'sanity-' + hash(newsQueries + JSON.stringify(route.query))
)
const { data } = await useSanityQuery(
  newsQueries,
  {
    topic: routeFilterTopic,
  },
  {
    key: queryKey, // successfully restored reactivity
  },
);
```

I suspect that when no key is provided, the string key `useSanityQuery` is constructing is based on the query and **the initial values of the parameters**, without reactivity. Under the hood, `useAsyncData` may be preventing a re-fetch because although the reactive parameters change, the key stays the same.

I also ended up fixing this by replacing useSanityQuery with a custom helper:

```ts
function resolveParams(params?: QueryParams): Record<string, unknown> {
  if (!params) return {};
  return Object.fromEntries(
    Object.entries(params).map(([paramKey, value]) => [paramKey, unref(value)]),
  );
}

export function useSanityAsyncData<T = unknown>(
  query: string,
  params?: QueryParams,
  options: AsyncDataOptions<T | null> & { key?: MaybeRefOrGetter<string> } = {},
) {
  const { client } = useSanity();
  const { key, watch, ...rest } = options;

  const queryKey = key ?? (() => `sanity-${hash(query + JSON.stringify(resolveParams(params)))}`);
  const paramWatchSources = params
    ? Object.values(params).filter(value => isRef(value) || typeof value === 'function')
    : [];

  return useAsyncData<T | null>(
    queryKey,
    () => client.fetch<T>(query, resolveParams(params)),
    {
      ...rest,
      watch: [...(watch ?? []), ...paramWatchSources],
    },
  );
}
```

This loses a ton of functionality provided by useSanityQuery, but it doesn't exhibit the reactivity issue that I was experiencing with useSanityQuery on routes with ISR enabled.

issues.body:
### The problem
When I started to use Nuxt + Sanity, I encountered a typegen problem. When I created queries, the types didn't generate and caused data to be shown with no type. So I couldn't use the typed data.

```
@nuxtjs/sanity WARN Could not generate Sanity types: Unknown file extension ".css" for node_modules\@sanity-labs\ui-poc\dist\styles.css
```

### The solution I'd like to see
The solution is to use `groq` without an import OR to use `defineQuery` with `groq` import

```TS
const myQuery = groq``
// or
import { defineQuery } from 'groq'

const myQuery = defineQuery(``)
```

----

### And another error

And another error is that when defining a Sanity schema, you'll have an error here


```TS
// studio/sanity.config.ts
export default defineConfig({
  // ...
  schema: {
    types: schemaTypes, // <--- 'types' field error
  },
})
```

At some point, I had to change the import from

`import {defineField, defineType} from 'sanity'` to `import {defineField, defineType} from '@sanity/types'`

BUT now it's causing an error, and I changed it back, and it fixed it.

So be aware of the import from `from '@sanity/types'` OR  `from 'sanity'`

### Repro

1. Add an image field with `options: { hotspot: true }` in the Studio schema.
2. Set a hotspot/crop on the image, off-center from the default.
3. Render it with `<SanityImage :asset-id="image.asset._ref" :alt="image.alt" w="..." h="..." />` — no `@nuxt/image` installed.
4. The rendered image ignores the hotspot/crop entirely and falls back to a plain center-crop.

### Why this isn't actually fixed by #714's resolution

#714 was closed as completed, pointing to `a79b73c` / #1255 ("render images with `<NuxtImg>` if available"), with "you may need to use nuxt/image".

I checked both code paths in the current release (`@nuxtjs/sanity@2.5.0`):

**`sanity-image-base.js`** (used without `@nuxt/image` installed):
```js
const src = computed(() => {
  if (!props.assetId) return "";
  const projectId = props.projectId || sanity.config.projectId;
  const dataset = props.dataset || sanity.config.dataset || "production";
  return buildImageUrl(props.assetId, projectId, dataset, props);
});
```
`buildImageUrl` only reads flat props (`rect`, `fpX`, `fpY`, `crop` (the enum), `w`, `h`, …) — it never looks at a source image's `hotspot`/`crop` object.

**`sanity-image-nuxt.js`** (used with `@nuxt/image` installed, i.e. the fix from #1255):
```js
const nuxtImgModifiers = computed(() => {
  const modifiers = {};
  for (const key of urlParamKeys) {
    const value = props[key];
    if (value !== void 0 && value !== null) {
      modifiers[key] = value;
    }
  }
  return modifiers;
});
```
This still just forwards the *same flat props* into `NuxtImg`'s `modifiers` — it doesn't derive `fp-x`/`fp-y`/`rect` from `hotspot`/`crop` either.

So installing `@nuxt/image` changes the rendering pipeline (adds responsive `srcset`, etc.) but does **not** solve the original report — the caller still has to manually compute `fp-x`/`fp-y`/`rect` themselves from the image's `hotspot`/`crop` fields, in either code path. That's a non-trivial bit of geometry (see below) that arguably belongs in this module, since it already owns `parseAssetId`/`buildImageUrl` and knows the asset's pixel dimensions.

### Proposed solution (no new dependency)

Let `<SanityImage>` optionally accept the image's `hotspot`/`crop` objects directly — either as two new props (`hotspot`, `crop`) or by accepting the whole Sanity image object — and derive `rect`/`fit=crop` internally in `buildImageUrl`, using the asset dimensions it already parses via `parseAssetId`. No need to depend on `@sanity/image-url` or `@nuxt/image`; the geometry is straightforward arithmetic on the 0–1 normalized `hotspot`/`crop` fields.

Algorithm (ported from `@sanity/image-url`'s `urlForImage`/`fit()`, adapted to emit this module's own `rect`/`fit` params instead of a finished URL):

```ts
export interface SanityImageHotspot { x?: number, y?: number, width?: number, height?: number }
export interface SanityImageCrop { top?: number, bottom?: number, left?: number, right?: number }

function parseAssetDimensions(assetId?: string | null) {
  const match = assetId?.match(/-(\d+)x(\d+)-/)
  return match ? { width: Number(match[1]), height: Number(match[2]) } : null
}

export function deriveCropParams(
  assetId: string | null | undefined,
  hotspot: SanityImageHotspot | null | undefined,
  crop: SanityImageCrop | null | undefined,
  targetWidth?: number,
  targetHeight?: number,
): { rect?: string, fit?: 'crop' } {
  const dimensions = parseAssetDimensions(assetId)
  if (!dimensions || (!crop && !hotspot)) return {}

  const { width: assetWidth, height: assetHeight } = dimensions
  const cropLeft = Math.round((crop?.left ?? 0) * assetWidth)
  const cropTop = Math.round((crop?.top ?? 0) * assetHeight)
  const cropRect = {
    left: cropLeft,
    top: cropTop,
    width: Math.round(assetWidth - (crop?.right ?? 0) * assetWidth - cropLeft),
    height: Math.round(assetHeight - (crop?.bottom ?? 0) * assetHeight - cropTop),
  }

  if (!targetWidth || !targetHeight) {
    const isEffectiveCrop = cropRect.left !== 0 || cropRect.top !== 0
      || cropRect.width !== assetWidth || cropRect.height !== assetHeight
    return isEffectiveCrop
      ? { rect: `${cropRect.left},${cropRect.top},${cropRect.width},${cropRect.height}` }
      : {}
  }

  const hs = hotspot ?? { x: 0.5, y: 0.5, height: 1, width: 1 }
  const hotspotCenterX = (hs.x ?? 0.5) * assetWidth
  const hotspotCenterY = (hs.y ?? 0.5) * assetHeight
  const hotspotRect = {
    left: hotspotCenterX - ((hs.width ?? 1) * assetWidth) / 2,
    top: hotspotCenterY - ((hs.height ?? 1) * assetHeight) / 2,
    right: hotspotCenterX + ((hs.width ?? 1) * assetWidth) / 2,
    bottom: hotspotCenterY + ((hs.height ?? 1) * assetHeight) / 2,
  }

  const desiredAspectRatio = targetWidth / targetHeight
  const cropAspectRatio = cropRect.width / cropRect.height
  let rect: { left: number, top: number, width: number, height: number }

  if (cropAspectRatio > desiredAspectRatio) {
    const height = Math.round(cropRect.height)
    const width = Math.round(height * desiredAspectRatio)
    const top = Math.max(0, Math.round(cropRect.top))
    const hotspotXCenter = Math.round((hotspotRect.right - hotspotRect.left) / 2 + hotspotRect.left)
    let left = Math.max(0, Math.round(hotspotXCenter - width / 2))
    if (left < cropRect.left) left = cropRect.left
    else if (left + width > cropRect.left + cropRect.width) left = cropRect.left + cropRect.width - width
    rect = { left, top, width, height }
  } else {
    const width = cropRect.width
    const height = Math.round(width / desiredAspectRatio)
    const left = Math.max(0, Math.round(cropRect.left))
    const hotspotYCenter = Math.round((hotspotRect.bottom - hotspotRect.top) / 2 + hotspotRect.top)
    let top = Math.max(0, Math.round(hotspotYCenter - height / 2))
    if (top < cropRect.top) top = cropRect.top
    else if (top + height > cropRect.top + cropRect.height) top = cropRect.top + cropRect.height - height
    rect = { left, top, width, height }
  }

  return { rect: `${rect.left},${rect.top},${rect.width},${rect.height}`, fit: 'crop' }
}
```

This is what I ended up hand-rolling as a userland utility to work around the gap (called manually before every `<SanityImage>` usage via `v-bind`), but it really feels like it belongs in the module itself, since:
- it only needs data the module already has (`parseAssetId`'s output),
- both rendering paths (`sanity-image-base.js` and `sanity-image-nuxt.js`) share the same gap and could both consume it,
- it doesn't add a runtime dependency — `@sanity/image-url` isn't even needed, just the arithmetic.

Happy to open a PR wiring this into `sanity-image-props.ts` (new `hotspot`/`crop` props, folded into `buildImageUrl`/`nuxtImgModifiers`) if that's a direction you'd take. Let me know if you'd rather see it shaped differently (e.g. a single `source`/`image` prop that accepts the whole Sanity image object instead of separate `hotspot`/`crop` props).

Related: #714

