issues.body:
### Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe
When an invalid SQL query is sent to the query API endpoint(`POST /__nuxt_content/[collection]/query`), the assertSafeQuery() function throws an error that is not properly caught. This results in an HTTP 500 Server Error being returned to the client, when it should return HTTP 400 Bad Request instead.
Query validation errors (client-side mistakes) should be treated as client errors (4xx) rather than server errors (5xx).

This is causing critical issues for production applications:
- Our application servers have monitoring systems that detect HTTP 5xx responses and trigger critical alerts. Invalid query validation errors are being incorrectly classified as server errors, causing our alert systems to fire even though there is no actual server problem.
- According to standard HTTP status code conventions and REST API best practices, validation errors on client input should return 400 Bad Request, not 500 Server Error. Returning 500 violates these conventions and makes it difficult for client applications to properly handle and differentiate between validation errors and actual server failures.


### Describe the solution you’d like
The error is thrown in src/runtime/api/query.post.ts, where `assertSafeQuery()` is called without error handling:
```typescript
export default eventHandler(async (event) => {
  const { sql } = await readBody(event)
  const collection = getRouterParam(event, ‘collection’)! || event.path?.split(‘/’)?.[2] || ‘’

  assertSafeQuery(sql, collection)  // ← Unhandled error becomes 500

  const conf = useRuntimeConfig().content as RuntimeConfig[‘content’]
  if (conf.integrityCheck) {
    await checkAndImportDatabaseIntegrity(event, collection, conf)
  }

  return loadDatabaseAdapter(conf).all(sql)
})
```

Wrap the `assertSafeQuery()` call in a try-catch block and return a proper 400 error response:
```typescript
  try {
    assertSafeQuery(sql, collection)
  } catch (error) {
    throw createError({
      statusCode: 400,
      statusMessage: ‘Bad Request’,
      data: {
        message: error instanceof Error ? error.message : ‘Invalid query’
      }
    })
  }
```

### Describe alternatives you’ve considered
`assertSafeQuery()` itself may throw error with `statusCode: 400` rather than wrapping the function.

### Additional context
- File affected: src/runtime/api/query.post.ts
- Function: assertSafeQuery() in src/runtime/internal/security.ts

## Environment

- OS: Windows 11
- Node: 24.21.0
- Package manager: npm 11.19.0
- Framework: Nuxt 4.5.2
- Affected package: @nuxt/content 3.16.1 (same code in 3.15.2)
- db0: 0.4.1 (nested under @nuxt/content)
- better-sqlite3: 13.0.3 (local cache database)

## Steps to reproduce

1. Build a project with markdown collections once, so that `_development_cache` gets populated:

```bash
npx nuxt build
```

2. Change some markdown files (or upgrade @nuxt/content, so that the collection/config hash changes) and build again:

```bash
npx nuxt build
```

## Describe the bug

When a cached entry exists but its checksum no longer matches, the build logs one `UNIQUE constraint failed: _development_cache.id` unhandled rejection per changed file. The build completes, but the cache rows for those files end up missing and they are parsed again on the next build, which then runs clean.

Root cause: `insertDevelopmentCache` calls `deleteDevelopmentCache(id)` without `await`, and `deleteDevelopmentCache` doesn't await its own `run()` either:

```js
const insertDevelopmentCache = async (id, value, checksum) => {
  deleteDevelopmentCache(id);
  const insert = generateCollectionInsert(cacheCollection, { id, value, checksum });
  for (const query of insert.queries) {
    await db.exec(query);
  }
};
const deleteDevelopmentCache = async (id) => {
  db.prepare(`DELETE FROM _development_cache WHERE id = ?`).run(id);
};
```

With the db0 better-sqlite3 connector, `prepare()` returns a wrapper whose `run()` first awaits `getDB()` and then the prepared statement (two awaits), while `exec()` awaits `getDB()` only once. So the `INSERT` executes before the `DELETE` and hits the existing primary key. The callers (`db.insertDevelopmentCache(...)` in the build loop and in the watcher) don't await or catch it either, so the rejection is unhandled.

Suggested fix:

```js
const insertDevelopmentCache = async (id, value, checksum) => {
  await deleteDevelopmentCache(id);
  // ...
};
const deleteDevelopmentCache = async (id) => {
  await db.prepare(`DELETE FROM _development_cache WHERE id = ?`).run(id);
};
```

(or a single `INSERT OR REPLACE`).

## Logs

```bash
 ERROR  [unhandledRejection] UNIQUE constraint failed: _development_cache.id

    at Database.exec (node_modules\better-sqlite3\lib\methods\wrappers.js:9:14)
    at Object.exec (/C:/projects/my-app/node_modules/@nuxt/content/node_modules/db0/dist/connectors/better-sqlite3.mjs:22:40)
    at async Object.insertDevelopmentCache (/C:/projects/my-app/node_modules/@nuxt/content/dist/module.mjs:361:7)
```

## Additional context

The error was printed 7 times in a single build (7 changed files) and disappeared on the following build. It doesn't break the output, but it is noisy and it silently drops the cache entries it was supposed to refresh. The same pattern is present in @nuxt/content 3.15.2.

### 🐛 The bug

When running `nuxt dev --public`, the devtools don't work when trying to access them from another computer on the same local network.

I depend on this because i always have the project running on one computer and access the site from a couple different computers on the same network.

### 🛠️ To reproduce

Run any project using devtools v4 via `nuxt dev --public` and try to open the devtools while accessing the website via the lan ip address.

### 🌈 Expected behavior

The devtools should expose the websocket to the same hosts nuxt itself does.

This may also affect people who depend on other flags like `--tunnel` or `--host` although I haven't tested that myself.

### ℹ️ Additional context

I had an agent write a pnpm patch to workaround the issue. Leaving it here in case it can help others.

