I have a new fix that works for me. I don't know what is causing this issue in only certain instances, but at least in my environment @vue/devtools-kit is only calling registerDevToolsPlugin for the first Vue app instance, which in Nuxt is the devtools overlay. This is why the VueElement segment in the render tree tab is selected by default and not the actual App 3. My simple fix currently is adding a new client only plugin:

```
/**
 * Workaround for @vue/devtools-kit only calling registerDevToolsPlugin
 * for the first Vue app instance. In Nuxt with devtools enabled, the
 * devtools overlay registers first, so Pinia's plugin setup is never
 * called for the main app. This forces the correct active app record
 * and triggers plugin registration.
 *
 * @see https://github.com/vuejs/devtools/issues/724
 */
export default defineNuxtPlugin({
  name: 'devtools-app-record-fix',
  enforce: 'post',
  async setup(nuxtApp) {
    if (!import.meta.dev) return

    nuxtApp.hook('app:mounted', async () => {
      try {
        const {
          setActiveAppRecord,
          setActiveAppRecordId,
          registerDevToolsPlugin,
          devtoolsAppRecords,
        } = await import('@vue/devtools-kit')

        const records = devtoolsAppRecords.value
        if (!Array.isArray(records) || records.length <= 1) return

        const appRecord = records.find((r: any) => r.app === nuxtApp.vueApp)
        if (!appRecord) return

        setActiveAppRecord(appRecord)
        setActiveAppRecordId(appRecord.id)
        registerDevToolsPlugin(nuxtApp.vueApp)
      }
      catch {
        // devtools-kit may not be available in all environments
      }
    })
  },
})

```

## Possible cause: a second Vue app mounting before the main one (check your Render Tree)

Hi! I ran into this exact issue (Pinia tab showing "No Data" while the app worked fine) and after a long debugging session I found a cause that I haven't seen mentioned here yet — sharing in case it helps someone.

**Quick check:** open Nuxt DevTools → **Render Tree** and look at the top — do you see **two apps** listed? In my case there were two, and when I selected the second one, all my Pinia stores appeared instantly. That also explains why the known workaround (clicking Render Tree → App → back to Pinia) works for some people: it's not refreshing anything, it's actually **switching the selected app record**.

**What's happening (as far as I understand):** Vue DevTools registers one "app record" per `app.mount()`, and the first app to mount becomes the selected one by default. If anything in your project mounts a secondary Vue app *before* the main Nuxt app (a toast container, a modal/overlay host, some widget inside a `.client.ts` plugin), that little app registers first — and the Pinia inspector ends up querying an app that has no Pinia installed. The stores were always there, just attached to the other app.

In our case it was a toast container plugin doing this during the plugins phase:

```ts
// the problem: mounts BEFORE the main Nuxt app
export default defineNuxtPlugin(() => {
  if (!import.meta.client) return
  const mountPoint = document.createElement('div')
  document.body.appendChild(mountPoint)
  const app = createApp({ render: () => h(ToastifyContainer) })
  app.mount(mountPoint)
})
```

**Fix that worked for us** — defer the secondary mount until the main app is mounted:

```ts
export default defineNuxtPlugin((nuxtApp) => {
  if (!import.meta.client) return
  nuxtApp.hook('app:mounted', () => {
    const mountPoint = document.createElement('div')
    document.body.appendChild(mountPoint)
    const app = createApp({ render: () => h(ToastifyContainer) })
    app.mount(mountPoint)
  })
})
```
After this, the Pinia tab opens correctly every time — no patches or downgrades needed.

If you're not sure what's creating the extra app, try searching your code and dependencies for `createApp(`. Common candidates: toast libraries, tooltip/modal hosts, embedded widgets.

This obviously may not be everyone's cause, but given how many reports mention the Render Tree workaround, I suspect it covers at least some of them. Hope it helps! 🍍

