**I was facing the same issue related to Nuxt trailing slash until I found a robust solution.**

**Since the trailing slash issue directly impacts SEO, it must be addressed promptly. But how?**

After much trial and error, I discovered that disabling `crawlLinks` and explicitly providing all URLs (with or without trailing slashes, depending on your preference) through the `routes` object effectively resolves the issue.

```javascript
nitro: {
  prerender: {
    routes: prerenderRoutes, 
    crawlLinks: false,
    trailingSlash: true, 
  },
  hooks: {
    async 'prerender:routes'(routes) {
      const allRoutes = []
      const genericPages = await fetchGeneric() 
      if (genericPages) {
        allRoutes.push(...genericPages) 
      }

      if (allRoutes.length) {
        for (const route of allRoutes) {
          routes.add(route) 
        }
      }
    },
  },
},

// ... (prerenderRoutes function remains the same)

// nuxt.config.ts
experimental: {
  viewTransition: false,
  defaults: {
    // Add trailing slash to nuxtLink defaults
    nuxtLink: {
      activeClass: 'nuxt-link-active',
      externalRelAttribute: 'noopener noreferrer nofollow',
      trailingSlash: 'append', 
    },
  },
},
```

I solved this problem this way
add a modules

```vue
import { defineNuxtModule, createResolver } from "@nuxt/kit";

export default defineNuxtModule({
  setup(options, nuxt) {
    //const { resolve } = createResolver(import.meta.url);

    // Hook into pages:resolved (rather than pages:extend) because:
    // 1. We want to modify routes after all other modifications are complete
    // 2. Some modules (like i18n) may override our changes if we use pages:extend
    // 3. This ensures our trailing slash addition is the final operation
    nuxt.hook("pages:resolved", (pages) => {
      pages.forEach((page) => {
        if (page.path.endsWith("/")) {
          return;
        }
        if (!page.file || !page.file.endsWith("index.vue")) {
          return;
        }

        page.path += "/";
      });
    });
  },
});

```

