> IMO This is expected and you can use <style scoped> (Scoped Styles) to avoid this.

@manniL You cannot use scoped styles to avoid this when the selector you're trying to set styles for cannot be scoped. For example, if I want one `body` to have a different background than another page (as is my use case), or if I want the `:root` to have a different value for a CSS variable, I'd have to jump through hoops using a script to do that, even though intuitively I should just be able to use a selector in a simple stylesheet, or a `:global` selector in scoped CSS.

This behavior not only leads to needless memory leaks and performance degradation but is also not expected or beneficial as far as I can tell. Plain HTML pages don't fail to unmount old styles after navigation, so why should Nuxt pages? It's surprising. I would understand if this issue just takes some time to fix due to a technical limitation (it does seem to be an issue of Vite mounting styles from unrendered Vue components in general), but to say this is the ideal behavior that makes sense, I don't see a justification for. Why would someone _want_ this behavior?

If this behavior for page styles is in fact ideal, then here's a follow-up question: what is the correct way to add a background to just one page? Because the following doesn't work due to this issue, and it's driving me crazy. You really shouldn't need a script for this!

