> I think this could be implemented as a compiler plugin that would early return from the `setup()` function after `navigateTo`.

Is someone working on this yet? Are there workarounds possible?

Besides the last `navigateTo` overwriting the previous one, it's very annoying that using `navigateTo` to force a redirect doesn't stop the setup script execution. It still requires you to make the page component compatible with the scenario where `navigateTo` is called. Example:

**Blog page: `pages/blog/[blogId].vue`**
```vue
<template>
  <div class="blog-item">
    <h1>{{ blog.title }}</h1>
    <div class="blog-item__contents" v-html="blog.body"></div>
  </div>
</template>

<script setup lang="ts">
  type Blog = {
    id: number
    title: string
    body: string
  }

  const route = useRoute()

  const { data: blog, error } = await useFetch<Blog>(`/posts/${route.params.blogId}`, {
    baseURL: 'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com',
    async onResponse ({ response }) {
      if (
        // This is a helper in /utils, obvious what it does :-)
        numberIsBetween(response.status, 300, 399)
        && 'type' in response._data
        && response._data.type === 'redirect'
        && response._data.to
      ) {
        console.log('Redirecting to:', response._data.to)
        await navigateTo({
          path: response._data.to,
        }, { redirectCode: response.status })
      }
    },
  })
</script>
```

Let's say the API would return the following object:
```json
{
  "type": "redirect",
  "to": "/posts/20",
  "statusCode": 301
}
```
With the current implementation of `navigateTo`, the page will error ("Cannot read property 'title' of undefined"), and users will see an error page instead of being redirected. One would need to add `v-if="blog.type !== 'redirect'` to the parent div of the page to make it work.

Just thinking out loud here, but Vue could provide an error class that can be thrown to let Vue know that we want to abort the setup script execution. This allows Nuxt to throw an error if we're in a setup script context, which we can check with `getCurrentInstance()`: if `null`, not a setup script context, otherwise it is. This way, Nuxt's `navigateTo` function can do something like:

```ts
import { SetupScriptAbortError } from 'vue'

async function navigateTo (...etc) {
  const instance = getCurrentInstance()
  if (instance !== null) {
    throw new SetupScriptAbortError(instance)
  }

  // Actually navigate
}
```

I have managed to fix this issue with the following code:

```ts
import type { RouteLocationRaw } from 'vue-router'

const useRedirectAndReturn = async (
  fn: (
    redirect: (args: RouteLocationRaw | undefined | null) => Promise<never>
  ) => Promise<any>
) => {
  const redirect = async (args: RouteLocationRaw | undefined | null) => {
    await navigateTo(args)
    throw new Error('Stop executing code')
  }

  try {
    await fn(redirect)
  } catch (error) {
    // Stop executing code
  }
}

export default useRedirectAndReturn

```

This is an usage example:

```ts
await useRedirectAndReturn(async (redirect) => {
  console.log('starts execution')
  if ([].length === 0) {
    await redirect({ path: '/' }, { redirectCode: 301 })
  }

  console.log('should not run')
})

```

I don't know if this is a good practice or not, but that seems to run in SSR and stop the execution by throwing an error.

I also ran into this problem for the same reason as  @Anoesj and others on here, but used a custom Error class & global plugin to solve the issue by catching a ForceRedirectError and navigating to the desired location.

utils/ForceRedirectError.ts:

```typescript
export class ForceRedirectError extends Error {
    constructor(message: string) {
        super(message)
        this.name = "ForceRedirectError"
    }
}
```


plugins/RedirectionErrorHandler.ts:
```typescript
import {ForceRedirectError} from "~/utils/ForceRedirectError";

export default defineNuxtPlugin(async (nuxtApp)=>{
    nuxtApp.hook('vue:error', async (error, instance, info) => {
        let vueError = error as Error
        if (vueError instanceof ForceRedirectError) {
            await navigateTo(vueError.message)
        }

    })
})
```

then in the component:
```html
<script setup>
const result = await loadSomething()

if (!result.success)  {
 throw new ForceRedirectError('/pageYouWant')
}
console.log('if result.success is false, this will not execute')
</script>
```

Seems to work without issue so far in both SSR and client modes, stops further execution of script setup in a component, and relies only on documented features.

issues.body:
### Describe the feature

Alexander Lichter made [a great video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aQPR0xn-MMk) about the new `getCachedData` function on `useFetch()` used for client-side caching. Looks great, and that inspired me to take it a bit further. Why not introduce a property `ttl` and `nuxt` can then do the rest for us under the hood.

The code Alexander wrote:
```js
const nuxtApp = useNuxtApp()
const { data } = await useFetch<any>('https://icanhazdadjoke.com/', {
  headers: {
    Accept: 'application/json'
  },
  transform(input) {
    return {
      ...input,
      fetchedAt: new Date()
    }
  },
  getCachedData(key) {
    const data = nuxtApp.payload.data[key] || nuxtApp.static.data[key]
    // If data is not fetched yet
    if (!data) {
      // Fetch the first time
      return
    }

    // Is the data too old?
    const expirationDate = new Date(data.fetchedAt)
    expirationDate.setTime(expirationDate.getTime() + 10 * 1000)
    const isExpired = expirationDate.getTime() < Date.now()
    if(isExpired) {
      // Refetch the data
      return
    }

    return data
  },
})
```

My suggested code would look like this
```js
const { data } = await useFetch<any>('https://icanhazdadjoke.com/', {
  headers: {
    Accept: 'application/json'
  },
  ttl: 10000
})
```

Where `ttl` is the time in millisecondes before the data get refetched. The `ttl` can then be used in `getCachedData` as Alexander calculated `isExpired` 🚀

### Additional information

- [ ] Would you be willing to help implement this feature?
- [ ] Could this feature be implemented as a module?

