Thanks @danielroe, that direction works well. I built a small proof of concept and the custom Nitro preset approach does recreate the old renderRoute behavior. Tested on Nuxt 4.4.8 (Nitro 2.13); the same API exists on Nuxt 3.

Here is the whole thing.

The custom preset just extends node-server and swaps the entry:

```
// preset/preset.ts
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'

export default {
  extends: 'node-server',
  entry: fileURLToPath(new URL('./entry.ts', import.meta.url)),
}
```
The entry exports a renderRoute function instead of starting an HTTP server:

```
// preset/entry.ts
import '#nitro-internal-pollyfills'
import { useNitroApp } from 'nitropack/runtime'

const nitroApp = useNitroApp()

export async function renderRoute(url = '/', options = {}) {
  const res = await nitroApp.localFetch(url, options)
  return { status: res.status, html: await res.text() }
}
```
Point Nitro at it:

```
// nuxt.config.ts
export default defineNuxtConfig({
  nitro: {
    preset: fileURLToPath(new URL('./preset/preset.ts', import.meta.url)),
  },
})
```
After nuxt build, any Node process can import the output and render in-process, no server listening:

```
import { renderRoute } from './.output/server/index.mjs'

const { status, html } = await renderRoute('/about')
```
That returned correct per-route SSR HTML for /, /about, and /blog/hello.

A few questions before I write the recipe:

Scope: is extending node-server and only overriding the entry (above) the right level for a good-first-issue recipe, or did you have a fuller standalone preset in mind?
Shape: should the recipe show renderRoute exported for another process to import (the original HCMS case, above), or an extra server route that returns HTML on request, or both?
defineNitroPreset: I first tried defineNitroPreset from nitropack/kit, but it failed to import during config loading (the ./kit export only has an import condition, so jiti/c12 couldn't resolve it under require). The plain object above worked. Is that import meant to work in user config, or should the recipe just use a plain object?
Location: should this live under Recipes, or alongside the existing rendering/deployment docs? Inline code only, or a companion example repo?
Happy to open the PR once I know your preferences.

