Unfortunately, it's almost impossible to preserve state from server -> client without having a key that matches.

However, with the new `useId` you can likely achieve exactly what you want with:

```ts
const ssrRef = (initialValue) => {
  const nuxtApp = useNuxtApp()
  if (import.meta.server || nuxtApp.isHydrating) {
    return useState(useId(), () => initialValue)
  }

  return ref(initialValue)
}
```

@danielroe Thank you for the suggestion!

I implemented my local `ssrRef` composable almost as your example, but I made `initialValue` a function, otherwise it will be calculated unnecessarily on hydration. I also added `onUnmounted` hook to automatically clear the value of the generated key when it is no longer required (I'm not sure about this as a `ref` could be shared between several components). And added `ssr-ref-` prefix to the generated `key` to prevent accidental collisions with other state keys (`useId()` returns fairly low-entropy, non-globally unique values such as `v-0-0`, `v-0-1`, so the probability of collision with some unrelated key (not generated by `useId()`) is high).

```ts
function ssrRef<T>(initialValue: () => T) {
  if (import.meta.server || useNuxtApp().isHydrating) {
    const key = `ssr-ref-${useId()}`
    onUnmounted(() => {
      clearNuxtState(key)
    })
    return useState(key, initialValue)
  }
  return ref(initialValue())
}
```

---

> it's almost impossible to preserve state from server -> client without having a key that matches

Yes, I understand this. But `useState` already solves this, right? It uses file/line if an explicit key is not set, if I'm not mistaken? I think this could be suitable for new `ssrRef` composable. It just needs to have different semantics, i.e. be re-initialized on every `setup()` execution, like a normal `ref`. And also it doesn't need to support of setting the `key` manually. It should **always** be generated, as it should not be possible to globally re-use such Refs.

Having a clean-up would also be good. But I'm not sure if it is possible to track if state/ref is not used anymore from **anywhere**.

