### Reproduction

[https://github.com/mrleblanc101/maison-du-marin-quebec/tree/ac778bcd370ffd8ce32895fe5cc5b38f87e49fdb](https://github.com/mrleblanc101/maison-du-marin-quebec/tree/ac778bcd370ffd8ce32895fe5cc5b38f87e49fdb)

### Describe the bug

Load the website and scroll the page.
Reload.
You'll be taken back to the top instead of staying at the current scroll position like a regular PHP website.

### Additional context

Claude tells me this:

> Root cause: Nuxt ships a default scrollBehavior on its router (for hash-link scrolling and page transitions).
> The moment any scrollBehavior is configured, Vue Router disables the browser's native
> history.scrollRestoration (vue-router.js:1162) — it wants to own scroll restoration itself. But Nuxt's
> default implementation only knows about a savedPosition for navigations it tracked within the current app
> session (back/forward inside the SPA); on the very first navigation after a fresh load or reload, there's
> nothing tracked yet, so it falls through to explicitly forcing { left: 0, top: 0 }. Net effect: native
> restoration gets turned off, and Nuxt then actively scrolls you to the top on every load. Apple.com doesn't
> do this (no SPA router overriding native restoration), so it just works.
>
> Fix: added app/router.options.ts, overriding Nuxt's default scrollBehavior to return false (do nothing)
> specifically for that first-navigation case, leaving whatever the browser already restored natively
> untouched. Everything else (hash-link scrolling, in-app route-to-route reset) is preserved as-is.
>
> Verified with a real browser: scrolled to scrollY: 1600, reloaded, landed back at 1600. Also confirmed the
> #donation/#shuttle CTA anchor scrolling still works correctly.

```
import type { RouterConfig } from 'nuxt/schema'
import { START_LOCATION } from 'vue-router'

// Same as Nuxt's built-in default (hash links get scroll-margin-aware
// smooth scrolling, in-app route changes reset to top), except for the
// very first navigation after a fresh load/reload.
//
// Configuring scrollBehavior at all makes Vue Router disable the browser's
// native history.scrollRestoration (it wants to own scroll restoration
// itself). Nuxt's default scrollBehavior then has no savedPosition to work
// with on that first navigation — it never tracked one, since this is the
// first navigation this app instance has seen — so it falls through to
// forcing scroll to (0, 0). That's what was overriding the browser's own,
// already-correct, natively-restored scroll position on reload. Returning
// false here instead leaves the browser's restoration in place.
export default <RouterConfig>{
    scrollBehavior(to, from, savedPosition) {
        if (from === START_LOCATION) return false

        if (to.path.replace(/\/$/, '') === from.path.replace(/\/$/, '')) {
            if (from.hash && !to.hash) return savedPosition ?? { left: 0, top: 0 }
            if (to.hash) return { el: to.hash, top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' }
            return false
        }

        if (savedPosition) return savedPosition
        if (to.hash) return { el: to.hash, top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' }
        return { left: 0, top: 0 }
    }
}
```

