Yes, I’m already aware that we can configure theme colors via AppConfig or override the CSS vars in `main.css`.

What I’m referring to is the case of **dynamically changing colors at runtime**, for example based on a user input (like what’s done in the ui docs with this [theme plugin](https://github.com/nuxt/ui/blob/v3/src/runtime/plugins/colors.ts)), or a color fetched from an API.

Right now, the only way to achieve this is to manually use `useHead()` to inject a `<style>` block with the updated values. It works, but it’s not super clean or intuitive.

```vue.js
<script setup lang="ts">
const { hostname } = useRequestURL();
const {data: platform} = await useFetch(() => `/api/platform/${hostname}`)

function generateColor(name: string, color: string) {
  return Object.entries(getColors(color))
      .map(([key, value]) => `--ui-color-${name}-${key}: ${value};`)
      .join('\n  ')
}

const root = computed(() => {
  return `@layer base {
  :root {
  ${Object.entries(platform.value.settings.colors).map(([key, value]) => generateColor(key, value)).join('\n  ')}
  }

  :root, .light {
  ${Object.entries(platform.value.settings.colors).map(([key, value]) => `--ui-${key}: var(--ui-color-${key}-500);`).join('\n  ')}
  }
  .dark {
  ${Object.entries(platform.value.settings.colors).map(([key, value]) => `--ui-${key}: var(--ui-color-${key}-400);`).join('\n  ')}
  }
}`
})

useHead({
  style: [{
    innerHTML: () => root.value,
    tagPriority: 'critical',
    id: 'my-colors'
  }]
})
```

